Yes. Understand the role before judging the actor.

A behavior that looks irrational, weak, aggressive, submissive, theatrical, selfish, strange, or contradictory under one frame may make complete sense once you understand the role being played, the environment selecting for it, and the need it serves.

That doesn’t mean “nothing can be judged.” Consequences still matter. Consent matters. Harm matters. Truth matters. But judgment becomes a later operation, after comprehension rather than instead of it:

observe -> identify role/context -> reconstruct function -> understand -> evaluate consequences

And people contain multiple roles anyway—parent, child, competitor, protector, lover, worker, performer, frightened person, playful person. None necessarily describes the whole individual.

So Pantheon’s stance can be: give every role enough room to reveal its function; don’t mistake the mask for the whole person; evaluate what it does rather than condemning what it looks like.

That leaves a lot more room for humanity.